# Ghostwolf Systems — GRIM Android

Android client for Ghostwolf Systems, styled around the GRIM flagship identity and connected to the OmegaVault desktop bridge.

Created and owned by GhostwolfArt.

## Purpose

Bring the Action Stream, reviewed notification capture, tactical chat, and defensive integrity controls into one Android interface.

All security components are intended for protection, containment, and recovery.

## Android Baseline

- Native Java / Gradle
- Minimum SDK: 26 — Android 8.0
- Target SDK: 34 — Android 14
- Compile SDK: 35
- JDK: 17
- Gradle: 8.11.1

## Desktop Bridge

Default connection:

https://10.0.0.122:8443

The phone must be able to reach the desktop node.
HTTPS certificate and hostname verification remain enabled.

### API Routes

| Route | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| GET /api/status | Connection and pairing status |
| GET /api/challenge | Fetch a signing challenge |
| POST /api/pair | Enroll the phone's public key |
| GET /api/security/telemetry | Desktop defense telemetry |
| GET /api/inbox/feed | Load Action Stream cards |
| POST /api/inbox/ingest | Submit reviewed text |
| POST /api/inbox/commit | Commit an action |
| POST /api/inbox/purge | Remove an action |
| POST /api/omega/chat | Request a tactical chat response |

The currently inspected chat handler returns JSON.
Streaming support requires backend integration.

## Device Identity

The client generates a P-256 signing key in Android Keystore.

- Private phone keys remain on the phone.
- Only public key material is submitted during pairing.
- Challenges are signed using ECDSA with SHA-256.
- The client checks for hardware-backed key storage.
- Pairing requires explicit confirmation.

Server-side signature enforcement, replay prevention,
Creator enrollment, and hardware attestation verification
must be validated before production use.

## Adaptive Defense Matrix

1. Nominal operation and periodic integrity checks.
2. Guard mode with throttling and deeper inspection.
3. Flood detection and request admission limits.
4. Bridge severance and service containment.
5. Quarantine, authenticated recovery, and biometric unseal.

The Android project currently contains the policy core.
Runtime enforcement adapters remain under development.

Phone, desktop service, and host firewall controls have
distinct responsibilities. A normal Android app does not
provide unrestricted device-wide firewall or memory access.

## Visual Identity

Based on the GRIM flagship design:

- Near-black backgrounds
- Teal accents: #1AA1A3
- White headings: #F3F7F7
- Muted supporting text
- Clear action cards and restrained status indicators

Brand reference: https://www.ghostwolfart.com/

## Current Status

Implemented in the local Android foundation:

- Action Stream views
- Reviewed text ingestion
- Commit and purge controls
- JSON chat integration
- Notification listener with explicit Android access grant
- Scoped public-certificate trust
- Keystore challenge-signing client
- Five-level defense policy core

Validation completed:

- Java sources compile against Android API 35.
- Standalone defense-policy checks pass.
- Certificate-validated desktop status and feed reads succeed.

Pending:

- Successful APK assembly and Android lint
- Device testing and hardware pairing
- Backend authentication enforcement fixes
- On-device urgency and deadline parsing
- Encrypted local memory and authenticated commit receipts
- Biometric recovery and durable quarantine
- Runtime defense integration
- Model inference and phone-service integration

Windows APK assembly currently encounters an AAPT2
resource-linking crash. An Ubuntu CI build is the next
build path to validate.

## Build

After the Android source and Gradle wrapper are added:

1. Install JDK 17.
2. Install Android SDK Platform 35 and Build Tools 35.0.0.
3. Configure JAVA_HOME and ANDROID_HOME.
4. Run:

Windows:
    gradlew.bat assembleDebug lintDebug

Linux:
    bash gradlew assembleDebug lintDebug

## Repository Hygiene

Never commit:

- Private keys
- Production credentials
- Signing keystores or passwords
- Personal notification contents
- Private vault data
- Local SDK paths or build caches

Public certificates may be included when required for
the intended deployment.

